  number={9},
  pages={4690-4702},
  abstract={This article presents a detailed investigation into optimizing the amplitude and phase of the transistor’s terminal voltages to generate a high 3rd-harmonic current in the millimeter-wave (mm-Wave) frequency. Based on the analysis, the digitally controlled source-combining triple-push (SCTP) oscillator is derived to significantly enhance the 3rd-harmonic current by introducing a large 3rd-harmonic component in the gate-to-source and drain-to-source voltages simultaneously. The analysis also aids in determining the resonant tank impedance and the output impedance. The proposed SCTP oscillator can achieve high output power and inherent rejection of 1st- and 2nd-subharmonic spurs without using multi-stage power- and area-hungry amplifiers or filters. Also, phase noise (PN) degradation due to the off-resonance oscillation in the triple-push oscillator is avoided by introducing auxiliary transconductance cells. Furthermore, the oscillator achieves high-frequency resolution by utilizing a linearized digital-to-current converter. Fabricated in 65-nm CMOS, the proposed 52.3-to-67.3-GHz SCTP oscillator prototype occupies a core area of 0.095 mm2 and consumes 24.8 to 32.8 mW. At a 60-GHz output frequency, the SCTP oscillator measures a PN of −126.17 dBc/Hz at 10 MHz, corresponding to a figure of merit (FoM) of 186.8 dBc/Hz. Benefited from a large frequency tuning range (TR) of 25.1%, the oscillator achieves the best-in-class FoMT at 1- and 10-MHz offsets. The measured frequency resolution is 35.8 kHz.},
  keywords={Oscillators;Solids;Current;Frequency;Voltage;Design methodology;Circuits;Transistors;Impedance;Waves; $3^{rd}$  -harmonic current enhancement;millimeter-wave (mm-Wave);phase noise (PN);source-combining;triple-push oscillator},
