  abstract={The analog front-end (AFE) often bottlenecks the power of modern receiver chains, burdened by the large-signal linearity required before the ADC. This work introduces a 77-dB DR, 200-MS/s two-stage SAR ADC that can alleviate much of this burden by offering a significantly lower input-referred noise (IRN) to enable low-gain AFEs, while reaching conventionally challenging speeds. This level of performance is achieved via several circuit techniques, including domain-crossing RC reference snubbers that passively suppress voltage ringing, which in turn enables a noise-efficient, open-loop floating-inverter residue amplifier (RA). Furthermore, an asynchronous pipelining scheme is uniquely utilized to minimize idle time and to enhance the backend precision, while delivering an 8-ns, sample rate independent conversion latency. The 40-nm ADC prototype demonstrates a 10.9-nV/ $\surd $ Hz NSD for 5.8 mW at 200 MS/s, and with a 179.4-dB FoMS,DR, it surpasses the noise and speed of prior low-noise, SAR-based ADCs.},
