  abstract={A high-precision, direct-digitization sensor readout (DD-RO) based on a continuous-time delta-sigma modulator (CT- $\Delta \Sigma $ M) is presented. The proposed DD-RO incorporates several key innovations to enhance performance. First, a pseudo-differential current-balancing integrator (PD-CBI) significantly extends the linear input range, whereas its intrinsically limited common-mode input range and CMRR are both improved by a common-mode cancellation loop (CMCL). Second, the PD-CBI enables quasi-quiet chopping for 1/ $f$  noise suppression and provides high input impedance. A multibit resistive DAC (RDAC) further increases the input impedance by reducing the transconductor’s net input voltage. Finally, an impedance-equalization technique employing auxiliary RDACs alleviates the signal-dependent nonlinearity arising from reference-path parasitics and DAC current variation. Fabricated in a 0.18- $\mu $ m CMOS process, the prototype achieves an input-referred noise (IRN) of 66 nV/ $\surd $ Hz, a 760 mVPP linear input range at 1.2 V supply, and a calibration-free input impedance of 7.83 G $\Omega $  and a CMRR of 82 dB, both measured at 50 Hz. The DD-RO achieves a peak SNDR of 103.6 dB and a dynamic range (DR) of 105 dB, corresponding to state-of-the-art figures of merit of FoM ${}_{\mathrm {SNDR}}= 173.5$  dB and FoM ${}_{\mathrm {DR}} = 174.9$  dB, respectively.},
