  number={9},
  pages={4841-4853},
  abstract={This work presents a CMOS-only [micro-electromechanical systems (MEMS)-free] electronic nose (e-nose) for concurrent multi-gas-sensing applications. The proposed system integrates 4096 capacitance-to-digital converter (CDC) pixels, each implementing a compact bandpass sigma–delta ( $\Sigma \Delta $ ) ADC with a single feedback coefficient and no additional feedforward or feedback paths, achieving each pixel footprint of only  $45\times 45~\mu $ m. The e-nose can achieve full baseline recovery and sensor reusability through an in-pixel heater to enable rapid and complete desorption of gas molecules from the sensing surface. The chip also includes 128 temperature-sensing pixels for on-chip thermal monitoring, utilizing a bandgap-based temperature sensor followed by a hybrid active–passive  $\Sigma \Delta $  ADC, maintaining the same compact pixel footprint as the CDC pixels. The system operates from a 1.1-V supply with a power consumption of  $10.3~\mu $ W per pixel at a 1-MHz sampling rate. To achieve high molecular selectivity, metal–organic framework (MOF) materials are utilized as the sensing interface. Two MOFs: CoNi–Pyz and HKUST-1 are employed for selective detection of ammonia (NH3) and isopropyl alcohol (IPA), respectively, on the same chip. For NH3 sensing, the proposed e-nose achieves a limit of detection (LoD) of 5 ppm, a sensitivity of 0.012 ( $\Delta $ C/C)/ppm, and a wide sensing range from 5 to 1000 ppm. To the best of authors’ knowledge, this work achieves the highest reported density for a CMOS solid-state e-nose and represents the first demonstration of integrating both single and multiple MOF transducer materials directly on a CMOS chip. It also establishes a thermodynamically modulated sensing approach enabled by the in-pixel heater, ensuring full sensor reset and reusability.},
