  abstract={This article presents GUARD, a fully digital, variation-tolerant detector for clock and voltage glitch attacks, featuring an integrated on-demand protection mechanism. Fabricated in 28-nm CMOS, GUARD provides robust security for digital systems by monitoring the system clock for maliciously injected faults. It employs a pair of optimized time-to-digital converters (TDCs) to detect clock pulsewidth variations as small as 100 ps and supports clock frequencies up to 1.2 GHz. Upon detection, a low-latency clock-gating mechanism is immediately triggered to prevent fault propagation into a victim microprocessor unit (MCU), achieving 100% protection success in experimental attacks. With simple one-time calibration, GUARD demonstrates robust operation across a wide industrial temperature range of  $- 40~{^{\circ }}$ C to  $125~{^{\circ }}$ C and a supply voltage range of 0.6 to 1.1 V. Furthermore, GUARD can differentiate between malicious voltage glitches and normal on-chip IR drops, thereby preventing unnecessary performance overhead. GUARD occupies  $2958~\mu $ m2 and consumes 0.874 mW per 100 MHz of operating frequency.},
