  abstract={This article presents a 5-to-5-V isolated DC–DC converter that suppresses radiated electromagnetic interference (EMI) using two control loops on symmetric transmitter/receiver (TX/RX) full-bridge stages. A complementary edge-aligned loop is implemented at the TX side to minimize fluctuations in the primary-side common-mode voltage (V ${}_{\mathrm {\!CM\_P}})$  during inverter switching transitions. An adaptive over-compensation loop is applied at the RX side to suppress secondary-side common-mode voltage (V ${}_{\mathrm {\!CM\_S}})$  fluctuation by enabling early turn-off near the zero-current crossing. These techniques directly reduce common-mode current (I ${}_{\mathrm {CM}})$  without relying on frequency hopping or external EMI suppression components. The transformer and feedback isolation capacitors are co-integrated in a four-layer low-cost package substrate and assembled with the TX and RX dies—fabricated in a 0.18- $\mu $ m BCD process—into a land grid array (LGA) package. The prototype achieves a peak efficiency of 60% and a maximum output power (P ${}_{\mathrm {OUT}})$  of 2.1 W. The system complies with CISPR-32 Class B radiated EMI limits on a two-layer PCB without using stitching capacitors or external beads.},
