  abstract={One-cycle-balance (OCB) response represents the optimal load-transient response for buck converters, maximizing system energy efficiency and robustness. While prior art has demonstrated OCB response for single load-step events, it fails in consecutive transients, which are prevalent in modern applications. This work overcomes these limitations by introducing an OCB controller that achieves a near-optimal response at every stage of a consecutive transient event sequence. The controller employs a polygon timer for one-cycle compensation of the accumulated charge imbalance. A relay emulated curve (REC) method achieves reduced-complexity sensing of the critical timing, independent of load current profiles in consecutive events. Moreover, a differentiator-based transient detector with dynamic boundaries successfully detects the onset of each transient event with low latency. Fabricated in a 0.18- $\mu $ m BCD process, a prototype demonstrates a nearly ideal OCB response, with a deviation-from-ideal (DFI) rate of less than 7.8% across various single and consecutive events.},
